WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Thanks for tuning into SpaceX Daily, episode 22.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.000
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.620
Today is July 4th, 2026.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.160
From the launch pad to the trading floor,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.980
here's everything that moved today.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.000
Starship engine tests clear a key hurdle

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.780
on the road to the 13th flight

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.660
and the first orbital refueling demonstration.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.120
Static fire runs at Starbase

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.260
confirmed six Raptor engines performed as expected

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.700
ahead of the next integrated flight test.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.560
The campaign continues the push toward full duration burns

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.740
required for orbital refueling.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.220
Data from these tests will feed directly

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.400
into vehicle software updates for the upcoming mission.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.620
Remember, the show covered Starship on yesterday's episode

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.340
where the focus remained on reuse cadence

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.180
and the orbital refueling demonstration timeline.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.640
Today's static fire data moves that forward

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.260
by validating integrated engine behavior

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.780
under flight-like conditions.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.460
Directly addressing the open question

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.640
of precise velocity changes during tanker to receiver docking.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.620
From an engineering standpoint,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.640
the recent Starship Engine Static Fire campaign

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.840
tests the integrated performance of six Raptor engines

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.940
under flight-like propellant conditions.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.000
The core constraint is delivering consistent chamber pressure

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.480
and mixture ratio across all engines

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.280
while managing thermal loads on the thrust structure.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.160
Each engine's turbo pump must maintain stable flow

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.260
without inducing vehicle-level pogo oscillations

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.900
that could damage the stack.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.680
Successful long duration burns reduce the uncertainty

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.420
around throttle response and shutdown sequencing

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.340
needed for the first orbital refueling demonstration.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.560
The data also informs production rate planning.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.260
If engine reliability holds at this scale,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.460
the bottleneck shifts from individual engine testing

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.240
to parallel vehicle assembly at both star base

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.220
and the emerging Florida sites.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.720
With Texas hardware now progressing,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.220
the program is also expanding its geographic footprint.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.320
Construction at Cape Canaveral continues

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.940
on dedicated Starship launch infrastructure

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.700
with pad modifications and propellant systems advancing

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.380
in parallel.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.460
SpaceX and contractors are targeting

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.560
an initial orbital class flight from the Florida site

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.700
before year end.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.920
This adds a second east coast launch option

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.000
beyond star base.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.420
Multiple sites along the space coast

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.960
are being prepared for Starship operations,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.640
including expanded ground support equipment

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.520
and potential tower infrastructure.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.880
The effort spreads launch cadence risk away

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.720
from a single Texas location.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.720
Early work focuses on vehicle processing

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.740
and propellant handling facilities.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.320
Observers on our slash SpaceX are debating

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.420
whether Florida's new pads will support simultaneous

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.700
Starship and Falcon operations

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.580
or required dedicated scheduling blocks.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.180
Separate threads examine the logistics

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.360
of moving fully stacked Starships

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.580
from Texas to Florida for early east coast flights.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.160
While SpaceX scales its own launch capacity,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.580
a direct broadband competitor is also nearing service.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.560
Amazon's project Cooper constellation

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.720
has reached the satellite count required

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.280
to begin commercial service.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.380
The first operational launches are scheduled

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.440
for the second half of 2026.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.940
This marks the first direct broadband competitor

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.760
with a comparable initial orbital population.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.680
Amazon has secured launch capacity

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.360
and completed enough satellites

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.700
to begin offering service in selected regions this year.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:38.940
The system will initially focus

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.060
on enterprise and government customers

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.780
before consumer expansion.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.520
Regulatory approvals for spectrum and ground stations

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.380
remain the next gating items.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.500
On the Falcon side, a different kind of milestone

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.460
was quietly passed this month.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.760
SpaceX has now completed its 1000th Falcon launch,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.740
a cumulative total across all variants since 2010.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.880
The milestone reflects sustained reuse rates

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.600
and rapid turnaround on the Falcon 9 fleet.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.480
NASA also awarded Rocket Lab

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.480
three dedicated electron missions

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.040
under existing contracts.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.560
The same Rocket Report covering that Falcon total

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.640
notes an Indian startup approaching

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.540
its debut orbital attempt.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.620
Coverage in the report places the Indian effort

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.980
on a near-term schedule

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.540
without naming the specific vehicle or pad.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.020
The item appears alongside the SpaceX count

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.180
as part of a broader weekly launch industry roundup.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.980
No additional technical details on the startup's propulsion

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.740
or payload plans are included in the summary.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.520
The report frames the Indian program

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.900
as one of several new entrants tracking toward first flight

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.920
in the current year.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.320
Observers following the article

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.700
are now watching national licensing steps

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.780
that typically precedes such a debut.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.000
Listeners have also been asking about access

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.320
to raw flight data.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.140
Community discussion centers on whether full resolution,

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.640
multi-camera raw footage from Falcon

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.500
and Starship flights will be archived publicly.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.340
Past releases have been limited to edited highlight reels.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.860
Observers note that uncut data

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.000
would aid independent thermal and structural analysis.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.980
Users are tracking contractor activity

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.400
at Cape Canaveral for signs of tower construction timelines

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.480
matching the reported 2026 target.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.020
A long-running discussion continues

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.160
on how raw telemetry from the recent engine tests

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.280
compares with prior Raptor iterations

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:37.700
in thrust stability.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.520
Looking farther ahead, one piece explores

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.060
what these capabilities could mean on century time scales.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.220
Long-range speculation in the piece examines

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.580
how Starship scale vehicles could alter

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.960
interplanetary logistics and in-space manufacturing.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.660
The analysis ties current reuse

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.340
and refueling milestones to potential century scale outcomes.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.000
No firm timelines are provided

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.040
beyond existing program goals.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:05.900
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.780
Amazon's Kuiper program has now assembled enough satellites

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.700
to begin service later this year,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:13.820
giving regulators and customers

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:17.220
a second operational LEO broadband network.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.860
This directly challenges StarLinks market position

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.660
in regions where both systems secure landing rights.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.540
Service debut hinges on successful initial launches

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.480
and ground segment certification.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.320
On the AI front,

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.440
no new source developments on orbital data centers

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.140
or XAI compute integration appeared in today's coverage.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.780
Watchers continue tracking StarLinks direct to sell

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.980
backhaul capacity and any future

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.500
cross ecosystem infrastructure announcements.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.340
And a quick market note,

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.560
SPCX closed at $162,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.080
up 2.6%.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.680
Before we go, Starship's Florida infrastructure push

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.020
keeps the reuse and refueling timelines

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.360
on parallel tracks.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.680
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.520
SPCX is trading at $162,

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.220
up 2.6%.

